    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey prof, i'm stuck on this signal processing stuff. i get the math of the fourier transform with the integrals and all, but i don't really get the actual intuition. like, what am I actually doing to a signal when i transform it? what does it even \"look like\" in terms of what's happening to the wave?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you apply the Fourier transform to a time-domain signal, you're essentially breaking it down into its constituent sinusoidal components, which are the distinct frequencies that make up the original waveform. Think of it as performing a \"frequency analysis\" on the signal. Each sinusoid carries both amplitude and phase information that corresponds to that element in the original waveform. After the transformation, you can think of your signal in the frequency-domain as a sum of weights on a spectrum of frequencies, where each weight represents the strength of a particular frequency component in your signal. This way, you’re literally translating the information from time to space dimensions. Data compression often leverages this property, where redundant or less important frequencies might be modified or discarded to save space while preserving the quality of the signal.",
      "source": "target",
